## SYNOPSIS

  shell
  shell <script.pds>
  shell -c '<command>'

The paideia-os interactive shell: reads user commands, spawns tool
processes with the caller's cap environment narrowed per each
callee's caps.decl, and threads text-and-schema-and-cap layers
through semantic pipes.

## DESCRIPTION

`shell` is the process the login supervisor launches after
authenticating a user. It reads a line from the terminal
(KIND_TTY), parses it into a pipeline of one or more child
commands, mints one KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT per pipe operator, narrows
the parent's capability environment against each callee's
caps.decl, invokes sys_execve, waits for exit, and appends a
HistoryEntry to `~/.history/`.

Every command execution is journaled to `/system/audit/user-
events/` via libpdx-audit BEFORE the child runs, and closed with
the exit code after wait. This is the D3 audit-first invariant:
a child cannot emit user-visible output until its
ShellCommandRecord is durable in the audit journal. A shell that
loses network to the audit-journal broker (`svc.audit-journal`)
refuses to spawn children — exit code 3 (system error) is the
correct response, and correcting the audit reachability is a
recovery operation the operator undertakes with intent.

The shell is a semantic-pipe conduit (D2 literal): a child's
schema-pipe output rides through the shell to the pipeline
consumer unchanged. The shell publishes three schemas of its
own: ShellPromptRecord (one per interactive prompt),
CommandCompletion (one array per tab-completion request), and
ShellCommandRecord (one per command exec + wait). These layer
alongside the forwarded child schemas over the same
KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT pair.

At session start, `shell` mints a KIND_SHELL_SESSION cap and
derives sub-caps per child (rights masked to READ | WRITE, not
MINT — the child cannot itself derive further sub-caps). The
session cap is the correlation id every audit-journal reader
uses to reassemble a pipe of tools as one unit of work.

## OPTIONS

  -c <command>              Execute <command> once and exit
  <script.pds>              Execute a .pds script and exit; see pds-format(5)
  --no-history              Suppress ~/.history/ writes for this session
  --no-cap:<KIND>           Strip the named capability at spawn time
                              (see plan.md I6; passed through to every child)

## EXAMPLES

  $ shell
  paideia $ ls
  ...
  paideia $ ls | cat > /tmp/files
  paideia $ exit

  $ shell -c 'grep foo /etc/hosts'

  $ shell ~/bin/build-report.pds

  $ shell --no-cap:KIND_PDXFS_FILE < deploy.pds
  shell: KIND_PDXFS_FILE stripped for this session
  ...

## FILES

  ~/.history/<session>-<ts>.pdxhist
    CoW-journal of the session's HistoryEntry records. Appended
    after every interactive line commit; format per history.pdx
    (24-byte header + UTF-8 command + zero pad to 8 bytes).

  /system/audit/user-events/
    Audit-journal path (owned by svc.audit-journal). One
    ShellCommandRecord per child command exec + wait.

  <cwd>/*.pds
    Script files the shell can execute directly. See pds-format(5)
    for the header conventions (shebang, capability manifest,
    schema imports, body).

## ENVIRONMENT

  Shell reads no environment variables. All configuration is
  either capability-driven (declared in caps.decl at build time
  and honoured at exec via the InitCap sidecar) or command-line
  driven (--no-history, --no-cap:*). This is deliberate — env
  vars are an ambient-authority channel this project avoids per
  plan.md D5.

## EXIT_STATUS

  0    Success — the last child exited 0 (or the shell exited cleanly).
  1    Caller error — the shell itself detected a bad flag / bad script.
  2    Usage — the shell was invoked with an unrecognised argument shape.
  3    System error — audit-journal unreachable, sys_execve failed,
       broker registration refused. See STATUS.md 0xFFFFECxx band.
  4    Capability denied — a required cap was stripped or not held.

## DIFFERENCES_FROM_POSIX

- No environment-variable configuration — POSIX shells read ~30 vars including PATH, PS1, IFS — paideia-os shell reads none; PATH is replaced by InitCap-seeded binary lookup and PS1 lives in a per-user config file the shell reads via KIND_PDXFS_FILE(read).

- Audit-first — POSIX shells write nothing to a durable audit trail before exec — paideia-os shell refuses to spawn until ShellCommandRecord is durable in svc.audit-journal.

- Capability handoff visible + refusable — POSIX shells silently propagate all inherited file descriptors and env — paideia-os shell narrows each child's cap set per that child's caps.decl and the user can strip further via --no-cap:<KIND>.

- Cap-declared IPC endpoints — POSIX pipes are anonymous file descriptors — paideia-os pipes are KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT caps with declared read/write rights and a target_ptr the child cannot forge.

- Schema-typed pipes — POSIX pipes carry bytes only — paideia-os pipes carry both bytes and a schema layer (D2); a downstream query tool reads schema-typed records without a re-parse from text.

